text:
What you will build
By the end, you will have followed the reasoning behind:

a pinned Conda environment;
a manually validated NGS workflow;
a strict Bash implementation;
a Docker/OCI container image;
Singularity execution on an HPC system;
a five-process Nextflow DSL2 workflow;
local and SLURM execution profiles;
failure tests, caching and output comparisons; and
a versioned, evidence-backed release.

How to use the book
Read the chapters in order the first time. Each practical chapter contains:

a biological objective;
an engineering objective;
a mental model;
small copyable commands;
expected output;
a verification checkpoint;
common failures and their causes; and
a short exercise.

When you reach the Nextflow section, run the repository’s five tiny examples in order. They progress from one value to paired FASTQs and a connected workflow before introducing real bioinformatics tools, containers and Slurm. Every example is executed automatically in continuous integration with the pinned tutorial version of Nextflow.
After the core learning path, the bonus real-world case study follows a multi-terabyte WGBS project from failed environment setup through a measured pilot, one complete-sample qualification and 30-sample capacity planning. It uses sanitized infrastructure and de-identified metrics and clearly separates measured results, projections and pending validation.

1.11 Nextflow: dependency and task orchestration
Nextflow is a workflow engine. We describe processes with input and output contracts. Channels carry values—such as sample identifiers and file paths—to processes. When all required inputs are available, Nextflow creates a task.
Nextflow can:

stage task inputs into an isolated work directory;
launch ready tasks without waiting unnecessarily for unrelated tasks;
request CPUs, memory and time;
run a task with Conda or inside a container;
submit tasks locally, to SLURM or to other executors;
record logs, traces, timelines and reports; and
reuse a matching successful task with -resume.

Nextflow does not decide whether Bowtie2 is biologically appropriate. It also does not replace Bowtie2: it coordinates the command that runs Bowtie2.
Question answered by Nextflow: Which task is ready, what are its inputs and outputs, what resources/runtime does it require, and can prior work be reused?

1.13 Git and GitHub: traceable change and collaboration
Git records snapshots of selected files and the relationships between those snapshots. It lets a team inspect what changed, why it changed and which exact revision was used. Branches allow work to be reviewed before it becomes part of the main history. Tags can name releases.
GitHub hosts Git repositories and adds collaboration services such as issues, pull requests, reviews, releases and automation.
Git should track source and small evidence artifacts:

Nextflow and Bash source;
environment specifications;
Dockerfiles;
configuration without secrets;
tests and documentation; and
small reports needed to support claims.

Git is generally a poor home for large FASTQ, BAM, index, cache and container files. Sensitive data must never be placed in a public repository.
Question answered by Git: Which source revision and documented decisions produced this project state?

1.20 Try it yourself
Draw the stack for one analysis you already know. It can be an R script, a microscopy workflow or a sequencing analysis. Identify:

input data;
analytical tools;
software environment;
ordering/dependencies;
execution location;
outputs and logs;
version-control evidence; and
acceptance criteria.

If a layer is absent, write “not yet controlled” rather than inventing it. That gap is useful information.

2.2 Engineering objective
This chapter builds a disposable practice workspace and a small evidence file. Nothing touches the bioinformatics pipeline yet. The goals are to:

know where you are;
know what a command will target;
inspect the result;
preserve error information; and
leave a reproducible record.

2.20 Investigate before deleting
This chapter does not require rm. That is deliberate.
Before any future deletion:

print the working directory;
resolve the exact target;
list the target;
confirm that it contains only disposable material; and
prefer a recoverable trash operation when practical.

Never use a recursive deletion command with /, ~, $HOME, an unresolved variable, a broad project root or an uninspected wildcard.

3.14 Hands-on exercise: audit a second execution context
Create a second report named context-comparison.txt.
Choose one comparison available to you:

a new terminal before and after conda activate base;
the workstation versus an HPC login session;
an HPC shell before and after loading a Java module; or
the host shell versus a container shell later in the book.

For both contexts, record:

uname -s and uname -m;
$SHELL and $0;
command -v for Git, Conda, Java and Nextflow;
the first three lines of java -version;
conda info --base when Conda exists;
whether Docker, Singularity/Apptainer and SLURM are expected there; and
one sentence explaining every changed executable path.

Finish with this conclusion template:
Context A is responsible for:
Context B is responsible for:
The most important PATH change was:
The change was expected because:
One missing command that is not an error is:






TipExercise verification hints





Use labeled command groups rather than copying an unstructured terminal history. Redirect Java’s standard error when capturing it:
{
  printf '=== CONTEXT A ===\n'
  command -v java || true
  java -version 2&gt;&1 | head -n 3
} &gt; context-comparison.txt
|| true prevents an expected missing command from stopping a larger strict script, but it also discards that failure status. Use it only when absence is explicitly part of the test.

starting a program is not the same as submitting a job;
requesting 32 CPUs does not make single-threaded software use 32 CPUs;
a job marked COMPLETED may still have produced invalid biological output;
a file can be on a large filesystem while the user’s quota is exhausted;
software can exist on the cluster but remain invisible until a module is loaded; and
activating Conda does not reserve any compute resources.

4.6 Login nodes are coordination nodes
Appropriate login-node work usually includes:

editing scripts and configuration;
inspecting small text files and job logs;
managing Git repositories;
submitting, monitoring and cancelling jobs;
transferring modest files through approved methods; and
performing lightweight environment checks.

Compute-intensive alignment, large compression jobs, broad recursive scans and workflow execution belong in an allocation. A login node is shared by many people; one uncontrolled process can degrade everyone’s session.
The line is set by local policy, not by a universal number of seconds or gigabytes.

4.18 Sanitize before sharing
Inspect:
less hpc-inventory.txt
Remove or replace:

internal hostnames if policy treats them as sensitive;
private project paths;
usernames when unnecessary;
account or allocation names not approved for publication; and
any accidental credential or patient-data reference.

Do not collect env, shell history, SSH configuration or key contents for this artifact.

4.21 Hands-on exercise: review a dangerous AI suggestion
An AI assistant proposes this command on a shared login node:
module load java
conda activate analysis
nextflow run main.nf \
  --reads '/project/patient-data/*.fastq' \
  -with-docker \
  -process.cpus 64 \
  -process.memory 240.GB \
  -resume &
Write ai-review.md with at least twelve findings. Classify each as:

site-policy unknown;
execution-model error;
resource assumption;
software-environment ambiguity;
data/privacy risk; or
verification gap.

Your review must address:

login node versus scheduler allocation;
whether Docker is available or permitted;
module version ambiguity;
module/Conda Java precedence;
unverified partition, account and QOS;
blanket CPU and memory requests;
quoting and wildcard responsibility;
protected-data path disclosure;
detached background execution;
-resume cache assumptions;
missing logs, reports and exit-status capture; and
absence of scientific output validation.

Then write a decision tree, not merely a corrected command:
If this is the login node → submit through the approved scheduler profile.
If the site supports Singularity → use the validated SIF/profile.
If Java comes from a module → verify its path and runtime before Nextflow.
If inputs are protected → follow approved storage and logging policy.
If resources are unknown → profile a small representative test first.






TipExercise reasoning hints





A strong review does not invent the correct partition or account. It identifies missing evidence and states how to obtain it. It also distinguishes operational success from scientific validation.
For every AI-generated line, ask:
What assumption does this line make?
Who owns that decision?
What evidence would falsify the assumption?
What damage follows if it is wrong?
How will success be verified?

a valid access key can still have dangerously broad permissions;
a bucket can contain the right bytes but be in the wrong account or Region;
a successful task can leave large intermediate files accumulating for months;
a container tag can silently point to different software later;
downloading data between Regions can cost money;
a budget notification can arrive after spending has occurred; and
technically correct infrastructure can violate consent, residency or institutional policy.

5.19 Practical acceptance checklist
The paid lab is complete only when you can show:

expected identity and Region were checked before resource creation;
a budget and personal spending ceiling were established;
only synthetic data was uploaded;
public S3 access was blocked;
upload/download bytes matched;
the Batch job reached SUCCEEDED and its log was inspected;
requested CPU, memory and image were recorded;
every lab resource was enumerated after teardown; and
delayed billing was checked later.

5.20 Hands-on exercise: design the next experiment
Design—but do not deploy—a cloud version of this book’s five-process pipeline: raw FastQC, Trim Galore, trimmed FastQC, Bowtie2 alignment/sort and BAM QC.
In cloud-nextflow-design.md, provide:

a diagram showing launcher, S3 prefixes, Batch, compute, registry and logs;
separate human, launcher and task identities;
proposed Region and the evidence needed to approve it;
input, work and result retention rules;
an immutable image reference strategy;
CPU and memory hypotheses for each process;
failure, retry and -resume behavior;
operational and scientific acceptance tests;
a cost model listing every potentially billable component; and
a teardown or production-ownership plan.

Then ask an AI system to review the design. Add a section titled AI claims I verified. For every accepted suggestion, identify the authoritative provider or Nextflow documentation, version and date checked. Reject suggestions that assume permissions, compliance approval or prices without evidence.

analysis.sh
analysis_fixed.sh
analysis_final.sh
analysis_final_really.sh
analysis_final_really_2.sh


what changed;
why it changed;
who changed it;
which version produced a result;
whether two files differ; or
how to return to a known revision.

6.9 What belongs in a bioinformatics repository?
Usually track small, reviewable files that define or explain the analysis:

Nextflow, Bash, Python or R source;
nextflow.config and nonsensitive parameter examples;
environment.yml, lock files and Dockerfiles;
small test fixtures when licensing and privacy permit;
checksum and provenance manifests;
tests, documentation and diagrams;
CI/CD workflow definitions; and
summary evidence small enough to review.

Usually do not track:

patient or participant data;
credentials, tokens, private keys or .env secrets;
large FASTQ, BAM, CRAM, index and reference bundles;
generated result directories;
Nextflow work/ and .nextflow/ state;
Conda environment directories;
Docker or Singularity image files; or
temporary/editor operating-system files.

“Git can store it” is not the same as “Git is the correct governed storage system for it.”

6.15 What Git does not guarantee
Git does not automatically prove:

scientific correctness;
authenticity of the author’s real-world identity;
compliance with privacy or regulatory policy;
that an external reference URL still serves the same bytes;
that ignored data is safely backed up;
that a remote copy exists;
that a container or result was built from the commit; or
that a commit was reviewed.

Those claims need additional controls and evidence: tests, checksums, provenance, signed artifacts, code review, governed storage and CI/CD.

21.10 Work is not results
work/ is Nextflow’s execution record and cache. A results directory contains selected outputs exposed for people and downstream use. Publishing does not mean that work files are disposable immediately: -resume needs the cache.
Keep these ideas separate:

input data are authoritative sources;
work data are task-level intermediates and cache;
published results are selected deliverables;
execution reports and logs are provenance evidence.

23.6 Observe without changing data
The example uses:
COUNT_LINES.out.counts.view { sample_id, count_file -&gt;
    "completed sample=${sample_id}; task_output=${count_file}"
}
view is a development aid. It displays items flowing through a channel and returns them unchanged. Do not print protected sample metadata into public or widely readable logs.

23.8 Why a samplesheet is still valuable
Filename pairing is concise when naming is perfectly regular. A production samplesheet is clearer when you need lanes, groups, library metadata, stranded status or paths that do not share a simple pattern.
Validate metadata before creating channels:

header matches the pipeline schema;
sample identifiers are unique under the intended grouping rules;
every R1 and R2 exists and is readable;
no orphan mate remains;
paths contain the intended sample, species and run;
protected identifiers are handled according to policy.

Nextflow can automate execution; it cannot infer that an incorrectly labeled FASTQ belongs to another patient.

24.7 Output contracts are scientific checkpoints
File existence alone is a weak acceptance criterion. Combine Nextflow contracts with format-aware checks:

paired trimmed FASTQs are nonempty and synchronized;
samtools quickcheck accepts the BAM;
BAM is coordinate-sorted and indexable;
FastQC reports exist for raw and trimmed data;
mapping summaries contain expected sample/reference context;
MultiQC or equivalent aggregation includes every intended sample.

A process can exit zero while producing scientifically unusable output. The manual baseline defines what “usable” means for the fixture.

25.5 The local profile
profiles {
    local {
        process.executor = 'local'
    }
}
Run:
nextflow run 05-two-step-workflow.nf -profile local
The controller and tasks run on the same computer. This is suitable for tiny development fixtures, not automatically for full WGS/WGBS datasets.

25.6 Labels and selectors
Give processes semantic resource classes:
process FASTQC_RAW {
    label 'small'
    // contract omitted
}
Configure the class:
process {
    withLabel: small {
        cpus = 1
        memory = 1.GB
        time = 10.m
    }
}
Labels keep resource intent out of repeated process code. withName targets a specific process and is useful when one expensive task needs an exception:
process {
    withName: BISMARK_ALIGN {
        cpus = 12
        memory = 72.GB
        time = 8.d
        maxForks = 10
    }
}
time is a termination ceiling, not a prediction. maxForks = 10 limits how many matching tasks run concurrently; it does not split a sample into ten.

25.7 Containers separate software from the host
A process can declare an OCI image:
process FASTQC_RAW {
    container 'quay.io/biocontainers/fastqc:0.12.1--hdfd78af_0'
}
Then a profile enables a compatible runtime:
profiles {
    docker_local {
        process.executor = 'local'
        docker.enabled = true
    }

    singularity_local {
        process.executor = 'local'
        singularity.enabled = true
        singularity.autoMounts = true
    }
}
The container supplies FastQC. Java still runs Nextflow on the host. Slurm still schedules tasks. Containers do not replace either layer.

25.8 Slurm plus Singularity
A sanitized profile has this shape:
profiles {
    slurm_singularity {
        process.executor = 'slurm'
        singularity.enabled = true
        singularity.autoMounts = true
        singularity.cacheDir = '/project/xxx/container-cache'
    }
}
Queue, account and cluster-specific options belong in a private site config:
process {
    queue = 'xxx'
    clusterOptions = '--account=xxx'
}
Do not publish private paths, accounts or internal topology. Provide a tracked example with placeholders and ignore the real local file.

26.6 Resume a matching run
Run the successful workflow again:
nextflow run 05-two-step-workflow.nf -profile local -resume
The status should report cached tasks. Nextflow reuses a successful task when its cache key still matches and its work data are available.
Changes that commonly invalidate a task include:

input content or metadata;
process script or relevant directives;
referenced executable/container configuration;
some environment or global variables used by the task;
cache mode and other relevant configuration.

Changing a README should not rerun an unrelated task. Changing the sample ID or command should.

26.9 Retries and resource escalation
Retries are appropriate for selected transient failures, not invalid FASTQs or wrong references. A common policy pattern is:
process {
    errorStrategy = { task.exitStatus in 137..140 ? 'retry' : 'terminate' }
    maxRetries = 2
    memory = { 8.GB * task.attempt }
}
Treat this as a design example, not a universal copy-paste policy. Confirm site exit-code meaning, node capacity and maximum resource limits. Record retries in the final evidence.

26.11 Verification checkpoint
Pass when:

the missing-input test exits nonzero before any task starts;
the unchanged rerun reports every eligible task as cached;
trace, report, timeline and DAG are nonempty;
required outputs remain valid; and
the run manifest records the command, versions, inputs and checksums.

27.4 Before you type
Confirm local site policy:

permitted partition/account and maximum wall-time;
compute-visible project/scratch storage;
Java and Singularity/Apptainer module names;
whether Nextflow itself may run on a login node;
container pull policy and cache location; and
scheduler submission-rate limits.

Use placeholders in tracked examples and keep real values in an ignored site configuration.

27.5 Site configuration
A private configuration might contain:
process {
    executor = 'slurm'
    queue = 'xxx'
    clusterOptions = '--account=xxx'

    withLabel: small {
        cpus = 2
        memory = 4.GB
        time = 2.h
    }

    withName: ALIGN_SORT {
        cpus = 8
        memory = 32.GB
        time = 24.h
    }
}

executor {
    queueSize = 30
    submitRateLimit = '2 sec'
}

singularity {
    enabled = true
    autoMounts = true
    cacheDir = '/project/xxx/singularity_cache'
}
queueSize limits tasks handled by the executor queue. submitRateLimit protects the scheduler from bursts. Process maxForks limits concurrent tasks matching a process/selector. These controls solve different problems.

27.9 Requested versus used resources
Slurm requests prevent task placement beyond declared limits; they do not prove software used those resources. Combine Nextflow trace and site accounting:
sstat -j TASK_JOB_ID.batch \
  --format=JobID,AveCPU,AveRSS,MaxRSS,MaxVMSize

sacct -j TASK_JOB_ID \
  --format=JobID,JobName,State,Elapsed,AllocCPUS,ReqMem,MaxRSS,ExitCode
Accounting availability and units vary by site. For a completed pilot, compare:

allocated CPUs with %cpu/CPU efficiency;
requested memory with peak RSS;
requested time with actual duration;
read/write I/O with storage planning;
retries and queue delays.

Keep headroom. Do not request a full node merely because it exists, and do not lower memory to the measured peak with no safety margin.

27.12 Verification checkpoint
Pass only if:

every intended process appears as a distinct Slurm task;
generated directives match reviewed CPU, memory and time policy;
task wrappers show the intended container runtime/image;
controller log ends with pipeline success;
trace native IDs map to scheduler evidence; and
required scientific outputs pass format and QC checks.

using the wrong reference index can invalidate alignment;
launching Java on a restricted login node can crash before Nextflow starts;
storing framework and image caches in a small home quota can corrupt downloads;
interpreting two Slurm task jobs as two samples can cause unnecessary intervention;
deduplicating independent chunks before merging can change the biological result;
deleting work/ too early can turn a recoverable interruption into a complete rerun; and
scheduler completion can be mistaken for a validated methylation dataset.

34.2 Case scope
The project contained 30 paired-end human WGBS samples:
30 biological samples
× 2 mates per sample
= 60 compressed FASTQ files
≈ 3.7 TB total compressed input
Individual FASTQs were approximately 47–88 GB. A representative pair was about 101 GB compressed with 151-base reads.
The required deliverables were:

raw and trimmed read QC;
Bismark alignment to GRCh38;
whole-sample duplicate removal;
coordinate-sorted BAM and BAI;
Bismark methylation coverage (.cov.gz);
Bismark reports and summary;
MultiQC; and
reproducible execution and provenance evidence.

The portable implementation used nf-core/methylseq 4.2.0, Nextflow, Singularity and Slurm.

34.5 Why the initial chunking plan was reconsidered
The first idea was:
split FASTQ into 10M-read chunks
→ align chunks
→ merge BAMs
→ split BAM by chromosome
→ extract methylation
Chunking alignment can reduce the wall-time of one enormous task, but it adds new correctness and operations obligations:

R1/R2 records must remain synchronized;
chunk identity must remain attached to the biological sample;
every alignment must use identical software, reference and parameters;
read groups and headers must be compatible before merge;
all chunks must be merged before one global sample-level deduplication;
missing or duplicate chunks must be detected;
merged and unsplit results require equivalence testing;
task count, I/O and temporary storage rise substantially; and
chromosome splitting can complicate paired reads and cross-contig behavior.

The dangerous design is to deduplicate each chunk independently and merge the results. Duplicates occurring in different chunks cannot see one another, so the final duplicate set may differ from whole-sample deduplication.
The default decision was therefore:

Process each biological sample as one paired dataset with the established nf-core/methylseq workflow. Consider chunked alignment only if measured complete-sample tasks exceed site limits, and merge before one global deduplication step.

This is an evidence-based default, not a claim that chunking is universally wrong.

34.16 Complete-sample acceptance gate
The case study was still running when this chapter was written. It must remain labeled in progress, and no complete-sample MultiQC conclusion should be claimed yet.
Acceptance requires:

controller log contains Pipeline completed successfully;
no failed or unresolved task remains;
deduplicated BAM and matching BAI exist and pass format checks;
Bismark .cov.gz exists, is nonempty and passes gzip -t;
Bismark sample report and summary exist;
MultiQC contains the intended sample and is reviewed;
trace records actual runtime, CPU, peak RSS and I/O;
total work/ and results/ storage are measured; and
private provenance/checksum manifest is complete.

Review MultiQC for at least:

raw and trimmed read quality;
adapter/quality trimming behavior;
mapping efficiency;
duplicate percentage;
methylation-extraction metrics; and
missing or unexpectedly duplicated samples.

Do not publish an unredacted report until sample identifiers, paths, command lines and infrastructure details have been reviewed for privacy.

34.17 Run manifests and checksums
For every pilot and production execution, retain a private manifest containing:

analysis purpose and operator;
controller and task identifiers;
repository commit and pipeline revision;
Java, Nextflow, container runtime and tool versions;
samplesheet and parameter-file copies;
SHA-256 checksums for those inputs;
reference source, release and FASTA checksum;
exact command and configuration layers;
exceptions/deviations;
trace/report/timeline/DAG paths;
required-output checks; and
QC review and approval status.

Checksums provide file-identity evidence. They do not prove that metadata are correct, a method is validated or an output is scientifically acceptable. In a regulated environment, integrate this engineering evidence into the applicable quality system rather than labeling a public tutorial “CLIA validated.”

34.19 What belongs in the public repository
Publish:

portable scripts and templates;
placeholder site configuration;
de-identified aggregate performance metrics;
assumptions and limitations;
scientific capacity figures; and
sanitized troubleshooting knowledge.

Do not publish:

credentials, tokens or private keys;
internal hostnames, accounts or topology;
institutional paths and support contacts;
raw FASTQs, BAMs or coverage files;
protected sample identifiers;
unreviewed MultiQC/logs containing private metadata; or
claims beyond the recorded evidence.

34.21 Decision record
The final production design was:

reuse nf-core/methylseq rather than reimplement Bismark orchestration;
use complete paired samples as the biological unit;
use containers for task software;
use Slurm per process task;
keep site paths and modules in ignored configuration;
pin workflow and Nextflow versions;
build one traceable index per species/assembly;
validate with smoke test, real-data pilot and one full sample;
cap Bismark alignment concurrency at ten for shared-resource fairness;
retain work/ until acceptance so -resume remains available; and
scale to 30 samples only after complete-sample MultiQC and resource review.
